 [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337356" :question/id #uuid "b4126f36-d39e-51f5-bf3c-6234278051da" :question/math-academy-id "q-337356" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=({x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337356/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337356"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337276/selection/c5f7ad4aac21b3b2132a0aac4e0a9c58df9e3dc147c764d68867830d09f400b6" :answer/id #uuid "f6c581de-c7f4-5505-86cc-e2877f1cf919" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337276/selection/c0e7c1cce9db7b9c5a4f8dcb9740c942e214cd9409449ab0d8a549180f591d80" :answer/id #uuid "085a4b8f-12c9-5023-82fe-a16ca10584c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^