859ae2611c2a4a43dab" "q-337362/selection/dde0bf389d957fd631eef608bf46a021f5d0660f339d2ec4393a93b040469c86"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337362" :question/id #uuid "f1ddbb09-cf38-595e-98d2-e5b17d8ef0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337362" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337362/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337362"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352895/field-1/731809e6967206c0c06d8bc301ac761310a988b1ff753feac3df89e794575d15" :answer/id #uuid "c9bdd279-d569-57b3-b8b1-f7ba9b5a920b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352895/field-1/f2d119ff0255695435eec5ab7ebcca19e1e2099822a0df6168ddf38dd3665a22" :answer/id #uuid