ype :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352895/field-3/1170e64b1af3b86b40b94a71544c416652dcbd56e960759dbe4588eda1e6a707" :answer/id #uuid "0b89e869-1375-56ec-b08a-36d0250e080a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352895/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "530b8a1d-784c-59ed-a7dc-86f1c19e2524" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-352895/field-3/b140585b3e79ed8a1f6eda2cc37a04d6e72a342ca4bb898f0b51ddecd0f585e1" :answer-field/choices ["q-352895/field-3/89c4b8b0175443c67f10fe44333b5dcdc044e4f64e5b1178b785557ee854890c" "q-352895/field-3/31e5118dec69c4bf1e70546778efb58bf5503877137f7b385ee12d3d3fb261d3" "q-352895/field-3/b140585b3e79ed8a1f6eda2cc37a04d6e72a342ca4bb898f0b51ddecd0f585e1" "q-352895/field-3/1170e64b1af3b86b40b94a71544c416652dcbd56e960759dbe4588eda1e6a707"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352895" :question/id #uuid "d21173e6-c5c3-5364-8e5b-d645bc7f05bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352895" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352895/field-1" "q-352895/field-2" "q-352895/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352891/field-1/0f0bf3e5b4d49d098cc5baf660c6977edc9ec113af6f735d59f497de50943951" :answer/id #uuid "ed04319b-b769-51cc-ac46-dd3cb775e57d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352891/field-1/90244fea567263d29a122e974f426c797e64ce018b292ff9ad249e079209d91d"