2288dd5166f8850f310a2ea3a2b2df07cc8e77ae476e23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337518" :question/id #uuid "777ddb07-aadb-556e-9de0-b0dc9c5809ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337518" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337518/field-1" "q-337518/field-2" "q-337518/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337518"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337466/field-1/f2d119ff0255695435eec5ab7ebcca19e1e2099822a0df6168ddf38dd3665a22" :answer/id #uuid "52d11c94-b79c-59db-88ea-5b09afb3cbce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337466/field-1/445f108130eddd660efb3f4e744c623ab48e31797df84645d9dabb690099bd49" :answer/id #uuid "29e186fa-1b2c-594c-93e2-0cc194a2133c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337466/field-1/58e66e13dc98313f1c71e73a32f04479429e770bf3852598ea62e9f4fbb454e6" :answer/id #uuid "5c43d78b-e262-54f3-9558-26c56304fb86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337466/field-1/104b19ddfff8532674993f9f432695275c1081fdb892f9b6b1664b5a8361b9ca" :answer/id #uuid "5022e34b-3eb8-54c0-b8b6-4b67a4472e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3