002ceb98" :answer/id #uuid "c920ef05-ede7-5346-b6d0-6a94e0213ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337466/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "35b1fa1c-7c5f-50f2-a9e1-2886f8233585" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337466/field-3/d8f31d2ae66fb8be8dde0e62ee776582712b274a2398e87a72759c67bd0b0694" :answer-field/choices ["q-337466/field-3/35583a6bcbd46b8baae2f82b9d05bb1b62cb5b7efdbe826a3cf1017082454f86" "q-337466/field-3/d8f31d2ae66fb8be8dde0e62ee776582712b274a2398e87a72759c67bd0b0694" "q-337466/field-3/857ff604e3435a2ba3a87cdd284e43ec807b478af64b9ffd4b9e865b5fecb259" "q-337466/field-3/981e09d4add050b9442c8c32c7557650a79b902f7b774b38502eb66e002ceb98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337466" :question/id #uuid "60c74d98-62e0-535f-ae2c-bd22a02ff3d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337466" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337466/field-1" "q-337466/field-2" "q-337466/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337466"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155427 :answer-field/correct 17592186155432 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155438 :answer-field/correct 17592186155440 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155433 :answer-field/correct 17592186155435 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337179"] :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =2+2{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =2+2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2+2{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-1/b17bc55297fcfa21ff8825c4fc77e14e581b1d9e6ce116e901996f6ba2e38511" :answer/id #uuid "a1c6a8f3-2457-5556-be60-f9bbb638cc22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-1/0dfd7211371cc7d8403c054fcef962b61d8a8feaf3d34d77257754f397cd581b" :answer/id #uuid "83b2a387-153e-5713-acb2-2d9eb8a6c3e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-1/0f0bf3e5b4d49d098cc5baf660c6977edc9ec113af6f735d59f497de50943951" :answer/id #uuid "d93b35aa-a255-5f3a-b92e-9a4884102d24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-1/1c87915a6c57b766bbb06d9fd3b05c238a68caca6be59893391c9af122458273" :answer/id #uuid "8c579bd9-165b-54a5-afa3-c14216f2ace2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-1/e912f68733f4a80eee8575a72c55c20feffdc1726bceb2f5d1304999aac87760" :answer/id #uuid "df338423-9736-55a3-8c81-85e4ebcd91e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9153fb9b-8b84-5b43-b227-1a6505270449" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337469/field-1/b17bc55297fcfa21ff8825c4fc77e14e581b1d9e6ce116e901996f6ba2e38511" :answer-field/choices ["q-337469/field-1/b17bc55297fcfa21ff8825c4fc77e14e581b1d9e6ce116e901996f6ba2e38511" "q-337469/field-1/0dfd7211371cc7d8403c054fcef962b61d8a8feaf3d34d77257754f397cd581b" "q-337469/field-1/0f0bf3e5b4d49d098cc5baf660c6977edc9ec113af6f735d59f497de50943951" "q-3