d6a3af7dc043f4c3e03da26a15440a9e9b19389783b2e1203bad6e37573" "q-337469/field-2/38dcd47d19959d51d8fdb19a88fcbf42b9c321773a93a752320fb8e9ac722eeb" "q-337469/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-337469/field-2/5dbc5a0aedeaaecf9bff216fd115adb3f549c2058284f4910eedf38e95a13c3c" "q-337469/field-2/60172a93ff8563c6527e61cddde086b162debe96b58dc56c2a5f96f012005c19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-3/38dcd47d19959d51d8fdb19a88fcbf42b9c321773a93a752320fb8e9ac722eeb" :answer/id #uuid "d7904e30-67d7-506d-8362-e53d77b92c12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-3/5dbc5a0aedeaaecf9bff216fd115adb3f549c2058284f4910eedf38e95a13c3c" :answer/id #uuid "6651a157-9404-56b4-851c-c3b23e4315cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-3/60172a93ff8563c6527e61cddde086b162debe96b58dc56c2a5f96f012005c19" :answer/id #uuid "0f0c6c53-1491-5590-a760-4fa8c8600e50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-3/0e0362d2084a1dc35fc11954d0a32439da98298eb4fd4b9f0d98c92ed9c2a7ac" :answer/id #uuid "e6e88ae0-40b5-5444-b469-bfe965901fb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b883cf93-0b66-598b-9c8d-150823fb8aee" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337469/field-3/0e0362d2084a1dc35fc11954d0a32439da98298eb4fd4b9f0d98c92ed9c2a7ac" :answer-field/choices ["q-337469/field-3/38dcd47d19959d51d8fdb19a88fcbf42b9c321773a93a752320fb8e9ac722eeb" "q-337469/field-3/5dbc5a0aedeaaecf9bff216fd115adb3f549c2058284f4910eedf38e95a13c3c" "q-337469/field-3/60172a93ff8563c6527e61cddde086b162debe96b58dc56c2a5f96f012005c19" "q-337469/field-3/0e0362d2084a1dc35fc11954d0a32439da98298eb4fd4b9f0d98c92ed9c2a7ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337469" :question/id #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337469" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337469/field-1" "q-337469/field-2" "q-337469/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337469"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-1/445f108130eddd660efb3f4e744c623ab48e31797df84645d9dabb690099bd49" :answer/id #uuid "90c2e1fa-1604-553d-b2f0-856ad232156a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-1/f2d119ff0255695435eec5ab7ebcca19e1e2099822a0df6168ddf38dd3665a22" :answer/id #uuid "8d4c1102-6c98-51e5-be16-44bb947b1ceb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-1/b32740e351355b70c938bf0539b16d220c4001c122a406bb6324609c9f042c67" :answer/id #uuid "bf4cb766-d883-5b1e-adec-bb846a0f298c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{12x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-1/4979f4e46ce15e233d9c3d358ad5f4fe6f678dd91b228ee79f31078c2e020125" :answer/id #uuid "a01ea5b4-24e7-5c53-918e-c81fbc9dbc2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-1/2f1558060bc7948e7df0f33b793d44036bcb59f27a8b7dfaf30575d55cc2a31c" :answer/id #uuid "72456b48-e075-5fe1-aa7e-9a04a84c0ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "943809b7-19a0-5707-b895-5b212b84b6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337510/field-1/445f108130eddd660efb3f4e744c623ab48e31797df84645d9dabb690099bd49" :answer-field/choices ["q-337510/field-1/445f108130eddd660efb3f4e744c623ab48e31797df84645d9dabb690099bd49" "q-337510/field-1/f2d119ff0255695435eec5ab7ebcca19e1e2099822a0df6168ddf38dd3665a22" "q-337510/field-1/b32740e351355b70c938bf0539b16d220c4001c122a406bb6324609c9f042c67" "q-337510/field-1/4979f4e46ce15e233d9c3d358ad5f4fe6f678dd91b228ee79f31078c2e020125" "q-337510/field-1/2f1558060bc7948e7df0f33b793d44036bcb59f27a8b7dfaf30575d55cc2a31c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-2/c64add59e1c2ac94814014888b688d0d377dcc424e245538573e95d6df05e1af" :answer/id #uuid "868935d0-e49f-5a6e-bf52-f103c83697de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-2/802178d41811a152feb493fcbf98b399bdd6bbe8cd41f51a062a9e3105b9194b" :answer/id #uuid "6dbe9943-b403-5b1b-8462-77d8b89d8bab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-2/e989a26cab8296543cc494ba2b103cf60e1b130d7854b629bf9792016c27f860" :answer/id #uuid "0496c7e0-37f8-5d13-8326-242f7bffe934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-2/14b1e95faecf905b87843dd162ebd24906b69f93833a397ad93a11c81a62972c" :answer/id #uuid "dbfb77dd-15bb-5b6a-91a7-0a2514ecdbe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a27eaf-57ee-53aa-ab93-27d8993f107c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337510/field-2/e989a26cab8296543cc494ba2b103cf60e1b130d7854b629bf9792016c27f860" :answer-field/choices ["q-337510/field-2/c64add59e1c2ac94814014888b688d0d377dcc424e245538573e95d6df05e1af" "q-337510/field-2/802178d41811a152feb493fcbf98b399bdd6bbe8cd41f51a062a9e3105b9194b" "q-337510/field-2/e989a26cab8296543cc494ba2b103cf60e1b130d7854b629bf9792016c27f860" "q-337510/field-2/14b1e95faecf905b87843dd162ebd24906b69f93833a397ad93a11c81a62972c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-3/7cca4671371c798106ba65a4b905bb9589473ee447c1b239638c844e7c37f281" :answer/id #uuid "e9a79a5c-4ce4-5635-b02f-41349e0916c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-3/14fe23213af1b149bae72f4e2f39f09484ec5cf1510d6a192111a0e28b64ad44" :answer/id #uuid "c1892694-8b47-5cf5-80a5-e72868e32a4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-3/6f21707d7ba23f9ee778fdfcd3617d1cf5fd300e1b3c88990a5e15b810ed8b61" :answer/id #uuid "ff71f8bd-f6db-5836-85e8-8c8f6758cbed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-3/7a22f59d2159ff302b25a9d27980960f4416e68c00a177d769c2dda74943a1f1" :answer/id #uuid "b81bf50c-18af-56fe-9e92-a8337b44c37e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cba6432-298f-5f3e-829c-84ecba257d9e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337510/field-3/7a22f59d2159ff302b25a9d27980960f4416e68c00a177d769c2dda74943a1f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-337510/field-3/7cca4671371c798106ba65a4b905bb9589473ee447c1b239638c844e7c37f281" "q-337510/field-3/14fe23213af1b149bae72f4e2f39f09484ec5cf1510d6a192111a0e28b64ad44" "q-337510/field-3/6f21707d7ba23f9ee778fdfcd3617d1cf5fd300e1b3c88990a5e15b810ed8b61" "q-337510/field-3/7a22f59d2159ff302b25a9d27980960f4416e68c00a177d769c2dda74943a1f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337510" :question/id #uuid "65f54963-6769-575e-9f24-5929927fa99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337510" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =6+6{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6+6{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6+6{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337510/field-1" "q-337510/field-2" "q-337510/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337510"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21774"] :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21775"] :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21777"] :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
